### Feature Description

When working with inputs that contain special tokens in the current models vocab (`<think>`, `<eos>` `<function_call>`, etc.) the true, special tokens are being fed into the model's input. This causes the model to read to those tokens, and thinking models will often reproduce those tokens directly in their reasoning blocks. This causes the auto-parser to get "confused" because there is no difference between these reproduced special tokens, and the actual ones used to drive the chat template. **The end result is seemingly "random" instances of generation just ending mid-thought, thinking leaking into the model response, or malformed tool calls.**

Adding a `--escape-special-in-input` or similar flag would eliminate this behavior when desired, but would preserve the current behavior for anyone who depends on it.

### Motivation

My understanding is that user input (and tool call results) should NEVER contain these special tokens, and they should instead be encoded as their BPE or char fallback variants, so that the model sees these tokens as normal text and not as the special tokens that drive the chat template. The current Jinja engine even contains references and full support for this technique as called out in the [Input Marking](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/common/jinja/README.md#input-marking) section of its README.

