### Summary

`common_peg_until_parser::operator()` in `common/peg-parser.cpp` treats
"reached the end of the input without ever finding the delimiter" as a
**successful** match when parsing a complete, non-streaming input
(`is_lenient() == false`). It should be a **failure** in that case — the
delimiter was never found, so the `until()` rule's precondition was never
satisfied. Currently it silently matches the entire remaining input as if
the delimiter didn't matter.

This is inconsistent with the sibling quoted-string parser earlier in the
same file (`common_peg_json_string_parser` or equivalent — see "Related
code" below), which already handles the identical situation correctly:
returning `COMMON_PEG_PARSE_RESULT_FAIL` when a closing quote never
appears in a non-lenient parse.

### Where this bites in practice

`common_chat_params_init_gemma4()` (`common/chat.cpp`) builds Gemma 4's
tool-call grammar using exactly this combinator:

```cpp
p.rule("gemma4-string-content", p.until("<|\"|>"));
p.rule("gemma4-string", p.literal("<|\"|>") + p.ref("gemma4-string-content") + p.literal("<|\"|>"));
```

When a model occasionally finishes generation without emitting the
closing `<|"|>` delimiter for a string value (independent of whatever
caused that — a separate concern), `common_chat_parse()`'s final,
non-lenient re-parse of the completed text should reject that tool call
as malformed. Instead, `until()` silently "succeeds," swallowing
everything up to end-of-input (or up to some unrelated, later occurrence
of the same delimiter from a completely different field) as if it were
the string's content. Depending on exactly where this happens, the
result is either:

- the tool call fails to parse at all further up the grammar (since
  whatever the parser now expects immediately after the string no longer
  matches what's actually there), or
- a `tool_calls[0].function.arguments` value that's well-formed enough to
  return from `common_chat_parse()` but is not valid JSON once a caller
  tries to parse it (a corrupted/truncated argument string).

Both outcomes trace back to the same root cause: `until()` never signals
failure here, so a broken generation is treated as if it parsed fine
until something downstream trips over the corrupted result instead.

### Root cause

`common/peg-parser.cpp`, `common_peg_until_parser::operator()`:

```cpp
common_peg_parse_result operator()(const common_peg_until_parser & p) const {
    common_trie matcher(p.delimiters);

    size_t pos = start_pos;
    size_t last_valid_pos = start_pos;

    while (pos < ctx.input.size()) {
        // ... scans forward, checking for a delimiter match at each position ...
    }

    if (last_valid_pos == ctx.input.size() && ctx.is_lenient()) {
        // Reached the end of a partial stream, there might still be more input that we need to consume.
        return common_peg_parse_result(COMMON_PEG_PARSE_RESULT_NEED_MORE_INPUT, start_pos, last_valid_pos);
    }
    return common_peg_parse_result(COMMON_PEG_PARSE_RESULT_SUCCESS, start_pos, last_valid_pos);
}
```

The loop exits having scanned the entire remaining input without a
delimiter match. If we're in lenient/streaming mode, `NEED_MORE_INPUT` is
correctly returned (more tokens might still be coming). But if we're
**not** lenient — i.e. this is a final parse of a complete, finished
generation — falling through to `COMMON_PEG_PARSE_RESULT_SUCCESS` is
wrong: there was no more input coming, and the delimiter still never
showed up, so the match should fail.

### Related code that already does this correctly

The quoted-string parser a few dozen lines above this in the same file
handles the identical case as expected:

```cpp
// Reached end without finding closing quote
if (!ctx.is_lenient()) {
    return common_peg_parse_result(COMMON_PEG_PARSE_RESULT_FAIL, start_pos, pos);
}
return common_peg_parse_result(COMMON_PEG_PARSE_RESULT_NEED_MORE_INPUT, start_pos, pos);
```

`until()`'s end-of-input branch appears to simply be missing the
equivalent `FAIL` case — this looks like an oversight rather than an
intentional difference in behavior between the two parsers.

### Minimal reproduction

Using the project's own PEG parser test harness (`tests/peg-parser/`),
following the pattern in `test-basic.cpp`:

```cpp
t.test("until_missing_delimiter_in_final_input_should_fail", [](testing & t) {
    auto parser = build_peg_parser([](common_peg_parser_builder & p) {
        return p.until("\"");
    });

    // Default-constructed context is non-lenient (a complete, final input,
    // not a streaming/partial one).
    common_peg_parse_context ctx("no closing quote anywhere in here");
    common_peg_parse_result  result = parser.parse(ctx);

    // Expected: false (FAIL) — the delimiter never appears.
    // Actual:   true  (SUCCESS) — the whole string is silently matched.
    t.assert_equal("until_missing_delimiter_should_fail", false, result.success());
});
```

### Suggested fix

```cpp
if (last_valid_pos == ctx.input.size()) {
    if (ctx.is_lenient()) {
        // Reached the end of a partial stream, there might still be more input that we need to consume.
        return common_peg_parse_result(COMMON_PEG_PARSE_RESULT_NEED_MORE_INPUT, start_pos, last_valid_pos);
    }
    // Final, complete input and the delimiter never appeared - this is a
    // genuine parse failure, not an implicit match to end-of-string.
    return common_peg_parse_result(COMMON_PEG_PARSE_RESULT_FAIL, start_pos, last_valid_pos);
}
return common_peg_parse_result(COMMON_PEG_PARSE_RESULT_SUCCESS, start_pos, last_valid_pos);
```

