# `gather_string_parts` merges string parts in O(N²); chat-template rendering collapses as tool count grows

### Summary

`jinja::runtime::gather_string_parts` (`common/jinja/runtime.h`) merges adjacent string parts by calling `vector::erase` inside a loop. Each erase shifts every following element, so an O(N) merge costs O(N²). Under callgrind this one loop accounts for **57% of all instructions** executed during a chat-template render.

The visible effect is that render time explodes with the size of the collections a template
iterates — most commonly `tools`. With the bundled
`CohereForAI-c4ai-command-r-plus-tool_use.jinja`, unmodified:

| tools | properties per tool | seconds | with the patch below |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 5 | 5 | 0.031 | — |
| 20 | 10 | 0.177 | — |
| 20 | 20 | 0.434 | **0.222** |
| 40 | 30 | 2.425 | 0.649 |
| 50 | 50 | 9.295 | **1.453** |
| 100 | 100 | 128.5 | **6.923** |

20–40 tools is an ordinary agentic setup, and it already costs half a second to two seconds of CPU per request before a single token is generated. The larger sizes are included to show where the curve goes, not because anyone sends them.

The patch produces **byte-identical prompts** at every size tested (26,521 / 149,971 / 587,721 bytes).

### The cause

```cpp
// common/jinja/runtime.h
static value_string gather_string_parts(const value & val) {
    value_string parts = mk_val<value_string>();
    gather_string_parts_recursive(val, parts);
    // join consecutive parts with the same type
    auto & p = parts->val_str.parts;
    for (size_t i = 1; i < p.size(); ) {
        if (p[i].is_input == p[i - 1].is_input) {
            p[i - 1].val += p[i].val;
            p.erase(p.begin() + i);      // <-- O(N) shift, inside the loop
        } else {
            i++;
        }
    }
    return parts;
}
```

`jinja::string` is a `std::vector<string_part>`. A render that emits N pieces produces N parts, nearly all carrying the same `is_input` flag, so nearly every iteration erases, each erase memmoves the tail. N merges cost O(N²) element moves.

callgrind, 20 tools × 20 properties:

```
54.31%  < caller: jinja::runtime::gather_string_parts        (15,460 calls)
36.39%    std::vector<jinja::string_part>::_M_erase          (35,121,435 calls)
20.84%    __memcpy_avx_unaligned_erms                        (the shifting)
```

`gather_string_parts` is also called 15,460 times for this one render, each call re-running the merge over the parts accumulated so far.

### Suggested patch

A single in-place compaction pass, O(N):

```cpp
// join consecutive parts with the same type (single in-place pass)
auto & p = parts->val_str.parts;
if (!p.empty()) {
    size_t w = 0;
    for (size_t r = 1; r < p.size(); r++) {
        if (p[w].is_input == p[r].is_input) {
            p[w].val += p[r].val;
        } else if (++w != r) {
            p[w] = std::move(p[r]);
        }
    }
    p.resize(w + 1);
}
```

The `++w != r` guard matters: without it the move degenerates into self-move-assignment whenever no merge has happened yet, and a self-moved `std::string` is left in an unspecified state. 
Our first attempt omitted it and silently lost 39 bytes of prompt, caught only because the reproduction below prints the rendered length.

The project's own tests pass with it applied: **`test-jinja`, `test-chat-template`, `test-chat-auto-parser` and `test-chat-peg-parser` all pass**. (`test-chat` does not build in this configuration — it needs `mtmd.h`, which `-DLLAMA_BUILD_TOOLS=OFF` excludes — and fails the same way without the patch, so it is unrelated.)

